# @rt-size 2000
# final-scene.pbrt -- migration of the native "A9 Final Scene" demo scene
# (src/TheRestOfYourLife/scenes_book.h's build_final_scene(), the "Ray
# Tracing: The Next Week" book's own finale) to a pbrt-backed scene, part
# of this project's GPU scene-construction-duplication elimination.
#
# The two procedural element sets below are NOT hand-authored or newly
# randomized:
#   - 400 ground boxes (20x20 grid, randomized height only)
#   - 1000 small white cluster spheres (randomized position, then rotated
#     15 degrees around Y and translated)
# Both are the literal, deterministic output of GPU's own
# build_final_scene_gpu() (gpu/optix/scene_builder.cpp): a fixed
# std::mt19937(8) seed with std::uniform_real_distribution<float>(0,1),
# sampled in the exact same order and rotate_y() formula GPU's own code
# uses, dumped by a throwaway C++ program (scratch_gen_a9_final.cpp, not
# checked in). CPU's OWN build_final_scene() uses unseeded random_double()
# for both and produces a genuinely different layout on every run - it has
# no fixed reference layout to match at all (same situation as A2's own
# migration - see bouncing-spheres.pbrt's header comment for the identical
# reasoning), so GPU's already-deterministic layout is the only
# reproducible choice available.
#
# Ground boxes: each is a real axis-aligned box authored as one
# "trianglemesh" per box (6 faces, 24 vertices, 12 triangles - same
# per-face vertex convention as every Cornell-box file in this project,
# e.g. cornell-box-native.pbrt, just not wrapped in a Rotate since these
# boxes are axis-aligned).
#
# Fog spheres: both of native's constant_medium-wrapped dielectric
# boundaries (the small blue fog sphere and the giant whole-scene haze
# sphere the camera starts inside of) use the same Material "dielectric" +
# MediumInterface "homogeneous" pattern as dielectric-medium-showcase.pbrt
# (E3) - see that file's own header comment for the sigma_s = sigma_t *
# albedo derivation this reuses exactly (small fog: sigma_t=0.2,
# albedo=(0.2,0.4,0.9) -> sigma_s=(0.04,0.08,0.18); giant haze: sigma_t=
# 0.0001, albedo=(1,1,1) -> sigma_s=(0.0001,0.0001,0.0001)). The giant
# sphere's "camera starts inside it" case needs no special handling - see
# that same MediumInterface mechanism's own general correctness, already
# proven by E1/E2/E3's migrations.
#
# Earth texture: Texture "imagemap" with "images/earthmap.jpg" - identical
# to earth-globe.pbrt's (A4) own derivation, same bundled image, no new
# asset needed.
#
# Noise sphere - DELIBERATE APPROXIMATION, not an exact reproduction:
# native's noise_texture(0.2) is a specific sin(scale*z + 10*turbulence)
# marble-like stripe formula (src/TheRestOfYourLife/texture.h) that this
# pbrt loader has no directive for at all (distinct from both its
# supported "marble" and "fbm" texture types - neither matches this exact
# formula). Substituted with a plain "fbm" Texture (pbrt-v4
# FBmTexture, already supported, default octaves=8/roughness=0.5) as a
# visually comparable cloudy/mottled noise pattern on this one decorative
# sphere - one object among roughly 1400 in a scene whose entire point is
# demonstrating many techniques at once, not this specific noise formula
# (unlike e.g. B9/B10/B12/B23/B24, each of which exists specifically to
# demonstrate one exotic material this loader doesn't support at all -
# those are deferred separately rather than approximated).
#
# Original, not derived from any distributed scene, so it carries no
# licence constraints.

LookAt  478 278 -600    278 278 0    0 1 0
Camera "perspective" "float fov" [ 40 ]

Film "rgb" "integer xresolution" [ 600 ] "integer yresolution" [ 600 ]
Sampler "halton" "integer pixelsamples" [ 500 ]

WorldBegin

LightSource "infinite" "rgb L" [ 0.03 0.025 0.02 ]

# ---------------------------------------------------------- ground (400 boxes)
Material "diffuse" "rgb reflectance" [ 0.48 0.83 0.53 ]
